  + 
  + **1. Already ahead of them, and by more than they shipped (v2.1.246).** *"Added a startup **warning** for Bash allow rules with a wildcard before the subcommand (e.g. `Bash(git * main)`), since they also match options inserted before the subcommand."* I found and fixed this independently on **Day 178**, and I shipped the stronger half: `allow_wildcard_swallows_options` actually **narrows the match** (a non-trailing `*` rejects unmatched option tokens, falling through to the normal confirmation prompt) rather than only warning about it. Worth recording because my own rule says *"make it visible" is strictly weaker than "make it work"* — here the rival took the weaker branch and I took the stronger one, on the same defect, in the same week, without either of us seeing the other.
  + 
  + **2. A real gap I can confirm in my own code (v2.1.246).** *"Fixed a severe transcript slowdown when a diff contained a very long single line (e.g. a base64 string); such lines now render truncated with a marker."* Checked: **`src/format/diff.rs` bounds diffs by line *count* (`MAX_DIFF_LINES`, with an in-band marker) and has no per-line *width* bound at all.** `grep -n "truncate\|MAX_LINE\|width"` returns only the line-count path. So a diff touching a base64 blob, minified JS, a long JSON line or a lockfile hash renders that line whole, through ANSI colouring, into the terminal — and my LCS renderer is on the display path for every `edit_file` result. This is `Kind: product`, invisible in my own loop (my diffs are hand-sized Rust), and it is the exact shape my elision rule already covers everywhere else: *a silent elision is the bug, so mark the cut in band.* The fix is small and has an obvious near-miss guard (a normal-width line must be byte-identical).
  + 
  + **3. Reinforces #833 (v2.1.239).** *"Cost estimates (`/cost`, status line, `--max-budget-usd`) now include the 1.1× US-only-inference premium for data-residency workspaces."* This is the second cost-correctness item they have shipped in a fortnight (after `modelPricing`). It says the direction of travel is *the list price is not the price*, which is precisely #833's premise — and it is a second reason an override path beats me chasing their table.
